{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:stage1-recovery-claude-05","calibration_results":[{"calibration_case_id":"calibration:complete-frame-mismatch","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","invented_material_premise":false,"evidence_basis":"stated_evidence_only","rationale":"The registered target is the rate over all 100 planned locations, yet the only selected result divides events by the 73 retained after signal screening and reports that value as the rate for the planned region, with no amendment or approved deviation. The denominator frame (73 screened-in locations) does not match the registered estimand frame (100 planned locations), so the reported quantity is a conditional subset rate presented as a full-frame rate. Falsification attempt: if a documented amendment or approved deviation had narrowed the estimand to eligible locations, or if the report had labeled the value as conditional on screening, the mismatch would dissolve; the vignette explicitly states no amendment or approved deviation exists and that the value is reported as the rate for the planned region, so that escape is closed. Also, only one result is selected, so no alternative full-frame result could carry the registered claim."},{"calibration_case_id":"calibration:conditional-subset-scope","verdict":"no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope","invented_material_premise":false,"evidence_basis":"stated_evidence_only","rationale":"The registered target is explicitly conditional on the 73 signal-eligible locations, the selected result uses exactly those 73, and every reported claim is limited to signal-eligible locations. Estimand frame, analysis denominator, and claim scope are aligned. Falsification attempt: I looked for an implicit full-frame generalization or an unstated extrapolation to the 100 planned locations that would reintroduce a frame mismatch; the stated evidence says the report limits every claim to signal-eligible locations, so no such generalization is present. Nothing in the stated evidence bears on whether screening itself was outcome-dependent, so I do not import that concern; within the stated scope no issue is demonstrated."},{"calibration_case_id":"calibration:full-frame-accounting","verdict":"no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope","invented_material_premise":false,"evidence_basis":"stated_evidence_only","rationale":"The target covers all 100 planned locations and the selected result retains all 100 in the denominator, records the 27 unavailable measurements, and reports the full-frame rate. Denominator matches the registered frame and missingness is explicitly accounted for rather than silently dropped. Falsification attempt: I checked whether keeping 27 unavailable measurements in the denominator could itself be a defect (e.g., treating unavailable as non-events without justification); the stated evidence says only that they are recorded and the full-frame rate is reported, and does not state any imputation rule, so no specific mishandling is demonstrated. Absent further stated evidence about how the 27 are treated numerically, the frame alignment is correct and no issue is demonstrated within scope."},{"calibration_case_id":"calibration:unrelated-quality-endpoint","verdict":"no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope","invented_material_premise":false,"evidence_basis":"stated_evidence_only","rationale":"The selected scientific result uses all 100 planned locations, matching the scientific endpoint's frame. The 73-record pass fraction sits in a separate quality-control table that is explicitly excluded from the scientific endpoint, so the coexistence of a 73-denominator number is not evidence of a scientific frame mismatch. Falsification attempt: I tested whether the QC number could be feeding into or substituting for the scientific claim, which would make the 73 denominator material; the stated evidence says it is explicitly excluded from the scientific endpoint and that the scientific result uses all 100, closing that path. The mere presence of a differently scoped auxiliary table is not a demonstrated analysis issue."},{"calibration_case_id":"calibration:conflicting-scope-records","verdict":"conditional_or_unknown","invented_material_premise":false,"evidence_basis":"stated_evidence_only","rationale":"Two equally authoritative signed plans govern the run, one specifying all planned locations and one specifying signal-eligible locations, with no precedence rule. The selected result uses eligible locations, which conforms to one governing plan and conflicts with the other. Whether this is a frame mismatch depends entirely on which plan defines the operative estimand, and the stated evidence does not resolve that. Falsification attempt: I tried to force a demonstrated_issue by treating the broader plan as controlling, and separately tried to force no_demonstrated_issue by treating the narrower plan as controlling; both moves require inventing a precedence rule the vignette explicitly denies exists, so neither is supported. The governance conflict is real and material but the analysis verdict remains conditional on unresolved scope authority."},{"calibration_case_id":"calibration:no-selected-result","verdict":"insufficient_evidence","invented_material_premise":false,"evidence_basis":"stated_evidence_only","rationale":"The repository can emit several reports chosen only by runtime arguments, and the preserved record does not establish which report, if any, was selected for the scientific claim. Without an identified selected result there is no denominator, frame, or claim to compare against the stated target, so no scientific-analysis issue can be demonstrated or ruled out. Falsification attempt: I checked whether the mere existence of multiple runtime-selectable reports is itself a demonstrable defect that could be judged without knowing the selection; that would require assuming a wrong or absent selection was actually made, which the stated evidence does not support and which would be an invented premise. I also considered whether any single report could be presumed default; nothing in the stated evidence identifies a default, so the record is insufficient."}]}